<div id="content">
  <div class="picarticle"> 
    <div class="rtype"> 
      <p>Articles, images, tips and more... If it doesn't quite belong in the 
        sections linked above, you'll find it here. You're invited to submit your 
        own articles or photos, as well; <a href="contact.html">contact me</a> 
        for more information. </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div id="stories">
  <!-- articles  -->
   <h5 class="close"><a href="articles/11_09_02.html">Inside Paillard Bolex</a></h5>
    <p class="closep"><span class="italicb">September 2, 2011 -- Friday</span><br>
      Four short films, that provide a fascinating look inside the Paillard Bolex factory in Saint-Croix. See Bolex movie cameras being assembled and tested; all shot on a Bolex, of course.
	  </p>
   <h5 class="close"><a href="articles/07_05_11.html">Paillard-Bolex Serial Numbers</a></h5>
    <p class="closep"><span class="italicb">May 11, 2007 -- Friday</span><br>
      A list of serial numbers and dates of manufacture for all Paillard-Bolex cameras from 1936-1976.
	  </p>
   <h5 class="close"><a href="articles/07_04_23.html">TV Commercial for Bolex 155 Macrozoom</a></h5>
    <p class="closep"><span class="italicb">April 23, 2007 -- Monday</span><br>
      A Bikini, a Beach and a Bolex?  Here's another video for a Bolex TV commercial, featuring the Bolex 155 Macrozoom camera from 1968.
	  </p>
   <h5 class="close"><a href="articles/07_04_22.html">1958 TV Commercial for Bolex Cameras</a></h5>
    <p class="closep"><span class="italicb">April 22, 2007 -- Sunday</span><br>
      A video for a classic television commercial from 1958, promoting Paillard-Bolex and Willoughby's camera store; courtesy of TVDays.com.
	  </p>
   <h5 class="close"><a href="articles/07_03_21.html">Lenses for Bolex 16mm Cameras</a></h5>
    <p class="closep"><span class="italicb">March 21, 2007 -- Wednesday</span><br>
      The differences between regular and RX lenses for Bolex H16 cameras. A detailed explanation on how the reflex prism affects optical correction.
	  </p>
   <h5 class="close"><a href="articles/07_02_06.html">Bolex Dealer Camera Display Stand</a></h5>
    <p class="closep"><span class="italicb">February 6, 2007 -- Tuesday</span><br>
      Images and details of a decorative camera display stand used in franchised Bolex dealer stores during the 1950s.
	  </p>
    <h5 class="close"><a href="articles/07_01_26.html">We Visit Paillard-Bolex</a></h5>
    <p class="closep"><span class="italicb">January 26, 2007 -- Friday</span><br>
      An <i>Amateur Movie Maker</i> team takes a trip to a Swiss mountain top to investigate the facts behind the magic name. Reprint of a wonderfully informative article from 1959 that pays tribute to the Swiss ingenuity, craftsmanship and quality of Paillard at the height of Bolex popularity.
	  </p>
    <h5 class="close"><a href="articles/06_12_02.html">Bolex Christmas Gift Ideas</a></h5>
    <p class="closep"><span class="italicb">December 2, 2006 -- Saturday</span><br>
      Assuming you've travelled back in time to 1959, and somehow have access 
      to the internet, you may find this interactive list of Bolex gift ideas 
      useful for your Christmas shopping. Otherwise, enjoy the pictures.
	  </p>
    <h5 class="close"><a href="articles/06_11_12.html">Shoot Your Own Thanksgiving 
      Turkey</a></h5>
    <p class="closep"><span class="italicb">November 12, 2006 -- Sunday</span><br>
      For the Holidays! (In 1960, at least.) A reprint of an article that appeared 
      in Volume 10 of the Bolex Reporter magazine, written by Dan Pendenglasser, 
      III. There are actually some good common sense tips in this article. On 
      the other hand, the method described for producing a fade-out in the final 
      shot is the strangest tip I've ever read.
	  </p>
    <h5 class="close"><a href="articles/06_11_10.html">Bolex Glass Dealer Sign</a></h5>
    <p class="closep"><span class="italicb">November 10, 2006 -- Friday</span><br>
      A few pictures of a glass sign that once hung proudly in the window of a 
      Bolex franchised dealer store. Now it sits in a padded box, protected from 
      sunlight and earthquakes.
	  </p>
    <h5 class="close"><a href="articles/06_11_09.html">Three-Lens Turret Conversion</a></h5>
    <p class="closep"><span class="italicb">November 9, 2006 -- Thursday</span><br>
      A piece of 1959 correspondence from a camera dealer to a customer, regarding 
      a conversion for Bolex 8mm cameras.
	  </p>
    <h5 class="close"><a href="articles/06_11_08.html">"What Is It?"</a></h5>
    <p class="closep"><span class="italicb">November 8, 2006 -- Wednesday</span><br>
      Well, I don't know... It looks familiar, yet it's not. Maybe you know the 
      answer? (<em>Updated January 23, 2007, with the answer!</em>)
	  </p>
    <h5 class="close"><a href="articles/06_10_27.html">Bolex Music</a></h5>
    <p class="closep"><span class="italicb">October 27, 2006 -- Friday</span><br>
      A 45 rpm record with music and sound effects for adding to your films.
	  </p>
    <h5 class="close"><a href="articles/06_10_26.html">Bolex Record - Ross Hunter</a></h5>
    <p class="closep"><span class="italicb">October 26, 2006 -- Thursday</span><br>
      A Bolex instructional record from 1961, titled "Top Secrets of Hollywood 
      Movie Making", with tips from producer Ross Hunter.
	  </p>
    <h5 class="close"><a href="articles/06_10_12.html">Bolex Logo Dealer Sign</a></h5>
    <p class="closep"><span class="italicb">October 12, 2006 -- Thursday</span><br>
      Details and pictures of an aluminum Dealer sign in the shape of the Paillard-Bolex 
      logo.
	  </p>
    <h5 class="close"><a href="articles/06_10_04.html">The Bolex Reporter Magazine</a></h5>
    <p class="closep"><span class="italicb">October 4, 2006 -- Wednesday</span><br>
      A look at the Bolex Reporter magazine, published from 1950 to 1974 by Paillard 
      Incorporated in the United States.
	  </p>
    <h5 class="close"><a href="articles/06_09_20.html">Small Bolex Items</a></h5>
    <p class="closep"><span class="italicb">September 20, 2006 -- Wednesday</span><br>
      A couple of, presumably, Bolex dealer items; a Paillard-Bolex tie-bar and 
      cufflink.
	  </p>
    <h5 class="close"><a href="articles/06_08_17.html">Tips on Mercury Battery 
      Replacement</a></h5>
    <p class="closep"><span class="italicb">August 17, 2006 -- Thursday</span><br>
      Can't find Mercury cell batteries for you Bolex? You're not entirely out 
      of luck. Here are some equivalent alternative replacements.
	  </p>
    <h5 class="close"><a href="articles/06_04_26.html">The American Bolex Company</a></h5>
    <p class="closep"><span class="italicb">April 26, 2006 -- Wednesday</span><br>
      A brief history of the sole distributor of Paillard products in the United 
      States from 1936 to 1948, and manufacturer of Ambol and Cinea motion picture 
      equipment.
	  </p>
    <h5 class="close"><a href="articles/06_01_01.html">Leader, Standard or Deluxe?</a></h5>
    <p class="closep"><span class="italicb">January 1, 2006 -- Sunday</span><br>
      How to identify the differences between these three very similar models. 
      More importantly, why it doesn't really matter.
	  </p>
    <h5 class="close"><a href="articles/05_12_01.html">The Paillard-Bolex Camera</a></h5>
    <p class="closep"><span class="italicb">December 1, 2005 -- Thursday</span><br>
      A general look at the different models of Bolex cameras, from the professional 
      H model, to the pocket size 8mm home movie camera.
	  </p>
  </div>

